Z||000000||new fiero||07-06-2001||01:17 AM||||I am rebuilding a caliper and purchased a rebuild kit for my 86 rear caliper.  Got everything back together to discover that the dust shield, the last piece, is too small.  I bought the kit from Autozone.  ANYONE else experienced this problem?<P>------------------<BR>newfiero<BR>(swapping 88 L4 to 86 and having fun!)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Seanh||07-06-2001||12:58 PM||||Are you absolutly sure its too small? Is the outside diameter too small to fit into the hole, or is the inside diameter too small for the piston? Thats why I don't like buying things like that from general parts stores. I did purchase mine at NAPA however, and they worked perfectly. I would have gotten them from the Fiero store, but I didn't feel like waiting on shipping when my Fiero was running with only front brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||new fiero||07-06-2001||08:49 PM||||Thanks for responding seanh.<P>Why is it that when you go to a parts store you get different answers from different people!<P>The lady behind the counter ordered the wrong kit.  Today I went in and they had 2 of the kit I needed on tuesday.  So I have the right kits now.  Might as well do both back calipers while the system is open...<BR><P>------------------<BR>newfiero<BR>(swapping 88 L4 to 86 and having fun!)||||reg||4
Z||000003||Brucepts||07-06-2001||08:58 PM||||I'm currently doing a rebuild also on my rear calipers, I was going to rebuild my old calipers. But when I priced rebuilt ones from Autozone for $31.99 a peice I deceided to buy  rebuilt ones. The ones I got looked exactly like the ones I was taking off, only thing I had to do is remove the ebrake bracket from one of the calipers and install it on the new one. For some reason the right rear one came rebuilt without it installed (actually had two come that way). I figured for that price it was small problem to deal with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> upon installation and brake bleeding it was also found that the hose had colapsed and was not letting fluid through to the caliper, so hose replacement is tomorrow . . . and then onto the ebrake adjustment<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||07-07-2001||11:57 AM||||Could someone with an autozone in their area please check and see how much they have the rebuilt front calipers for? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||new fiero||07-07-2001||12:36 PM||||The kits were only 5.50 each and unbelievably easy to rebuilt once I got started.  The majority of the time was cleaning the calipers themselves.  Thanks to all who have posted.  I never got any reply on fiero.org's mail list.<P>------------------<BR>newfiero<BR>(swapping 88 L4 to 86 and having fun!)||||reg||6
Z||000006||theogre||07-07-2001||12:37 PM||||The front ones should be cheap at most places. You can check autozone prices and others online.<P>napaonline.com<BR>autozone.com<BR>partsamerica.com<BR>I forget checker/shuck/kragens site....<P>Keep an eye on those rebuilt rears... sometimes problems with them don't show up right away.<P>Just how rebuildable a rear caliper is depends on a couple things...<P>1. piston. if the piston has the silicone "plug" and fluid is comming out of it, replace the caliper. There is a seal inside the piston that can't reliably be fixed w/o special tools. (If you can even get the seal.)<P>2. Bleeders. Before even trying this make sure they open. Other wise replace the caliper and let somone at the rebuilder deal with it.<P>3. Banjo. As we've seen in other threads, they can strip the hole easy. This problem may not show up right away. if the hole is crappy or you didn't get them to proper tourque they can back off and start leaking.<P>Like I said, just keep an eye on them... no matter who rebuilt them. The pro ones don't always go perfect either.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||07-07-2001||01:48 PM||||I have the little rebuild kits for my front calipers, but these things are stock calipers with 122K on them, and the piston is rusty and probally not in good working order. Can I buy just new pistons somewhere? I checked all those places on-line, but I don't see any calipers (front) under $51.99 each. I very highly doubt the front are going to be more expensive then the rears, anyplace. I already rebuilt my rears, and they are workin' great. Well, until the "red-headed step-child" took care of the front of my car that is. I have the entire front end apart now, so I thought I would rebuild and paint them now. I'm having a *****  of a time trying to get the piston out even. I tried compressed air, but only got a face full of brake fluid (not nice on the eyes at all, be careful if you try doing it this way). I was told to just hook them back up to the brake lines and pump the pedal a few times and they should pop out. Will that work? All the fluid is drained from the system, so I think I'll need to pour some in the tank but do I actually have the bleed the brakes, or will the air push the caliper out just as well? Thanks!||||reg||1
